//www.opensecrets.org/elections-overview/cost-of-election","series_or_table":"Total Cost of Election (1998-2024), adjusted for inflation","accessed":"2026-10-18","manual_check":true},"verified":{"method":"fetched","evidence":"| 2002 | $3,800,687,543 | $3,800,687,543 | N/A |"}},{"id":"money.election_cost.total.2000","module":"money","metric":"Total cost of federal elections (candidates, parties, and outside groups, inflation-adjusted)","value":5615211015,"congressional_races_usd":3040886225,"presidential_race_usd":2574324789,"unit":"USD (inflation-adjusted)","period":"2000","geo":"US","party_context":{"president":"D","senate":"R","house":"R"},"plain_english":"Total federal election spending in the 2000 cycle reached $5,615,211,015 (adjusted for inflation), with $3,040,886,225 spent on congressional races and $2,574,324,789 on the presidential race.","source":{"tier":"B","publisher":"OpenSecrets","title":"Total Cost of Election (1998-2024)","url":"https://www.opensecrets.org/elections-overview/cost-of-election","series_or_table":"Total Cost of Election (1998-2024), adjusted for inflation","accessed":"2026-10-18","manual_check":true},"verified":{"method":"fetched","evidence":"| 2000* | $5,615,211,015 | $3,040,886,225 | $2,574,324,789 |"}},{"id":"money.election_cost.total.1998","module":"money","metric":"Total cost of federal elections (candidates, parties, and outside groups, inflation-adjusted)","value":3118941069,"congressional_races_usd":3118941069,"presidential_race_usd":null,"unit":"USD (inflation-adjusted)","period":"1998","geo":"US","party_context":{"president":"D","senate":"R","house":"R"},"plain_english":"Total federal election spending in the 1998 cycle reached $3,118,941,069 (adjusted for inflation), with $3,118,941,069 spent on congressional races (a midterm cycle with no presidential election).","source":{"tier":"B","publisher":"OpenSecrets","title":"Total Cost of Election (1998-2024)","url":"https://www.opensecrets.org/elections-overview/cost-of-election","series_or_table":"Total Cost of Election (1998-2024), adjusted for inflation","accessed":"2026-10-18","manual_check":true},"verified":{"method":"fetched","evidence":"| 1998 | $3,118,941,069 | $3,118,941,069 | N/A |"}},{"id":"money.court_case.buckley_v_valeo.1976","module":"money","metric":"Supreme Court ruling: Buckley v. Valeo, 424 U.S. 1 (1976)","value":"Upheld contribution limits and disclosure; struck down limits on candidate, personal, and independent expenditures","unit":"ruling","period":"1976-01-30","geo":"US","party_context":{"president":"R","senate":"D","house":"D"},"plain_english":"On January 30, 1976, the Supreme Court ruled in Buckley v. Valeo that spending money to influence elections is protected speech: it kept limits on direct donations to candidates and required donor disclosure, but struck down limits on how much candidates or independent individuals can spend.","source":{"tier":"A","publisher":"Federal Election Commission","title":"FEC Legal Resources: Buckley v. Valeo","url":"https://www.fec.gov/legal-resources/court-cases/buckley-v-valeo/","series_or_table":"Case summary","accessed":"2026-10-18"},"verified":{"method":"fetched","evidence":"On January 30, 1976, the Supreme Court issued a per curiam opinion in Buckley v. Valeo... The Court upheld the constitutionality of certain provisions... limitations on contributions to candidates for federal office (2 U.S.C. §441a); The disclosure and recordkeeping provisions... The Court declared other provisions of the FECA to be unconstitutional, in particular: The limitations on expenditures by candidates and their committees... The $1,000 limitation on independent expenditures... The limitations on expenditures by candidates from their personal funds"}},{"id":"money.court_case.citizens_united_v_fec.2010","module":"money","metric":"Supreme Court ruling: Citizens United v. FEC, 558 U.S. 310 (2010)","value":"5-4 ruling struck down bans on corporate and union independent expenditures and electioneering communications","unit":"ruling","period":"2010-01-21","geo":"US","party_context":{"president":"D","senate":"D","house":"D"},"plain_english":"On January 21, 2010, the Supreme Court ruled 5-4 in Citizens United v. FEC that corporations, nonprofits, and labor unions can spend unlimited money from their own treasuries on ads and other independent spending for or against federal candidates, as long as they do not coordinate with a campaign.","source":{"tier":"A","publisher":"Federal Election Commission","title":"FEC Legal Resources: Citizens United v. FEC","url":"https://www.fec.gov/legal-resources/court-cases/citizens-united-v-fec/","series_or_table":"Case summary","accessed":"2026-10-18"},"verified":{"method":"fetched","evidence":"On January 21, 2010, the Supreme Court issued a ruling in Citizens United v. Federal Election Commission overruling an earlier decision, Austin v. Michigan State Chamber of Commerce (Austin), that allowed prohibitions on independent expenditures by corporations. The Court also overruled the part of McConnell v. Federal Election Commission that held that corporations could be banned from making electioneering communications. The Court upheld the reporting and disclaimer requirements for independent expenditures and electioneering communications."}},{"id":"money.court_case.speechnow_v_fec.2010","module":"money","metric":"D.C. Circuit en banc ruling: SpeechNow.org v. FEC, 599 F.3d 686 (2010)","value":"9-0 en banc ruling struck down contribution limits to independent-expenditure-only groups, creating Super PACs","unit":"ruling","period":"2010-03-26","geo":"US","party_context":{"president":"D","senate":"D","house":"D"},"plain_english":"On March 26, 2010, the D.C. Circuit Court of Appeals applied Citizens United in SpeechNow.org v. FEC and struck down limits on how much money individuals can donate to groups that only make independent expenditures, creating modern Super PACs that can raise and spend unlimited sums.","source":{"tier":"A","publisher":"Federal Election Commission","title":"FEC Legal Resources: Speechnow.org v. FEC","url":"https://www.fec.gov/legal-resources/court-cases/speechnoworg-v-fec/","series_or_table":"Case summary","accessed":"2026-10-18"},"verified":{"method":"fetched","evidence":"On March 26, 2010, the D.C. Circuit Court of Appeals held that the provisions of the Federal Election Campaign Act that limit the contributions that individuals may make to SpeechNow.org, and the contributions that SpeechNow.org may accept from them, violate the First Amendment... In light of the Supreme Court’s recent decision in Citizens United v. FEC... the appeals court ruled that \"contributions to groups that make only independent expenditures cannot corrupt or create the appearance of corruption.\""}},{"id":"money.court_case.mccutcheon_v_fec.2014","module":"money","metric":"Supreme Court ruling: McCutcheon v. FEC, 572 U.S. 185 (2014)","value":"5-4 ruling struck down biennial aggregate limits on total individual contributions to federal candidates, parties, and PACs","unit":"ruling","period":"2014-04-02","geo":"US","party_context":{"president":"D","senate":"D","house":"R"},"plain_english":"On April 2, 2014, the Supreme Court ruled 5-4 in McCutcheon v. FEC to strike down the two-year overall cap on how much one donor could give to all federal candidates, party committees, and PACs combined, allowing wealthy donors to write single checks of hundreds of thousands of dollars to joint party-candidate fundraising committees.","source":{"tier":"A","publisher":"Federal Election Commission","title":"FEC Legal Resources: McCutcheon, et al. v. FEC","url":"https://www.fec.gov/legal-resources/court-cases/mccutcheon-et-al-v-fec/","series_or_table":"Case summary","accessed":"2026-10-18"},"verified":{"method":"fetched","evidence":"On April 2, 2014, the Supreme Court issued a ruling in McCutcheon v. FEC that struck down the aggregate limits on the amount an individual may contribute during a two-year period to all federal candidates, parties and political action committees combined. By a vote of 5-4, the Court ruled that the biennial aggregate limits are unconstitutional under the First Amendment."}},{"id":"money.vote.bcra_2002.house_passage","module":"money","metric":"House passage roll-call vote on Bipartisan Campaign Reform Act of 2002 (McCain-Feingold / Shays-Meehan, H.R. 2356)","value":"Passed 240-189 (D: 198-12, R: 41-176, I: 1-1)","vote_totals":{"yea":240,"nay":189,"not_voting":6,"D":{"yea":198,"nay":12,"not_voting":1},"R":{"yea":41,"nay":176,"not_voting":5},"I":{"yea":1,"nay":1,"not_voting":0}},"unit":"roll_call_vote","period":"2002-02-14","geo":"US","party_context":{"president":"R","senate":"D","house":"R"},"plain_english":"On February 14, 2002, the House passed the Bipartisan Campaign Reform Act (McCain-Feingold / Shays-Meehan, H.R. 2356) to ban unlimited soft money donations to national political parties by a vote of 240-189: Democrats voted 198-12 in favor, Republicans voted 41-176 against, and Independents split 1-1.","source":{"tier":"A","publisher":"Office of the Clerk, U.S. House of Representatives","title":"107th Congress, 2nd Session, Roll Call Vote 34 on H.R. 2356","url":"https://clerk.house.gov/evs/2002/roll034.xml","series_or_table":"Roll Call 34","accessed":"2026-10-18"},"verified":{"method":"fetched","evidence":"<rollcall-num>34</rollcall-num><legis-num>H R 2356</legis-num><vote-question>On Passage</vote-question><vote-result>Passed</vote-result><action-date>14-Feb-2002</action-date>... Republican: yea 41, nay 176, not-voting 5; Democratic: yea 198, nay 12, not-voting 1; Independent: yea 1, nay 1; Totals: yea 240, nay 189, not-voting 6"}},{"id":"money.vote.bcra_2002.senate_cloture","module":"money","metric":"Senate cloture vote on Bipartisan Campaign Reform Act of 2002 (McCain-Feingold, H.R. 2356)","value":"Cloture invoked 68-32 (D: 50-0, R: 17-32, I: 1-0)","vote_totals":{"yea":68,"nay":32,"not_voting":0,"D":{"yea":50,"nay":0,"not_voting":0},"R":{"yea":17,"nay":32,"not_voting":0},"I":{"yea":1,"nay":0,"not_voting":0}},"unit":"roll_call_vote","period":"2002-03-20","geo":"US","party_context":{"president":"R","senate":"D","house":"R"},"plain_english":"On March 20, 2002, the Senate voted 68-32 to end debate (invoke cloture) on the Bipartisan Campaign Reform Act (McCain-Feingold, H.R. 2356), clearing the 60-vote threshold: Democrats voted 50-0 in favor, Republicans voted 17-32 against, and the Senate's 1 Independent voted yes.","source":{"tier":"A","publisher":"U.S. Senate Legislation & Records","title":"107th Congress, 2nd Session, Senate Roll Call Vote 53 on H.R. 2356","url":"https://www.senate.gov/legislative/LIS/roll_call_votes/vote1072/vote_107_2_00053.xml","series_or_table":"Roll Call Vote 53","accessed":"2026-10-18"},"verified":{"method":"fetched","evidence":"vote_number 53, March 20, 2002: Cloture Motion Agreed to (68-32, 3/5 majority required); member party tally: D Yea 50, D Nay 0; R Yea 17, R Nay 32; I Yea 1, I Nay 0"}},{"id":"money.vote.bcra_2002.senate_passage","module":"money","metric":"Senate passage roll-call vote on Bipartisan Campaign Reform Act of 2002 (McCain-Feingold, H.R. 2356)","value":"Passed 60-40 (D: 48-2, R: 11-38, I: 1-0)","vote_totals":{"yea":60,"nay":40,"not_voting":0,"D":{"yea":48,"nay":2,"not_voting":0},"R":{"yea":11,"nay":38,"not_voting":0},"I":{"yea":1,"nay":0,"not_voting":0}},"unit":"roll_call_vote","period":"2002-03-20","geo":"US","party_context":{"president":"R","senate":"D","house":"R"},"plain_english":"On March 20, 2002, the Senate passed the Bipartisan Campaign Reform Act (McCain-Feingold, H.R. 2356) by a vote of 60-40, sending it to President George W. Bush to be signed into law: Democrats voted 48-2 in favor, Republicans voted 11-38 against, and 1 Independent voted yes.","source":{"tier":"A","publisher":"U.S. Senate Legislation & Records","title":"107th Congress, 2nd Session, Senate Roll Call Vote 54 on H.R. 2356","url":"https://www.senate.gov/legislative/LIS/roll_call_votes/vote1072/vote_107_2_00054.xml","series_or_table":"Roll Call Vote 54","accessed":"2026-10-18"},"verified":{"method":"fetched","evidence":"vote_number 54, March 20, 2002: Bill Passed (60-40); member party tally: D Yea 48, D Nay 2; R Yea 11, R Nay 38; I Yea 1, I Nay 0"}},{"id":"money.vote.disclose_act.2010_cloture_1","module":"money","metric":"Senate cloture vote #1 on DISCLOSE Act of 2010 (S. 3628, motion to proceed)","value":"Rejected 57-41 (D: 56-1, R: 0-40-1 NV, I/ID: 1-0-1 NV)","vote_totals":{"yea":57,"nay":41,"not_voting":2,"D":{"yea":56,"nay":1,"not_voting":0},"R":{"yea":0,"nay":40,"not_voting":1},"I":{"yea":1,"nay":0,"not_voting":1}},"unit":"roll_call_vote","period":"2010-07-27","geo":"US","party_context":{"president":"D","senate":"D","house":"D"},"plain_english":"On July 27, 2010, a Senate motion to end the filibuster on the DISCLOSE Act (S. 3628), requiring dark money groups and super PACs to disclose major donors, failed 57-41 (60 votes needed): Democrats voted 56-1 in favor (Reid switched to No to move to reconsider), Republicans voted 0-40 against (1 not voting), and Independents had 1 yes and 1 not voting.","source":{"tier":"A","publisher":"U.S. Senate Legislation & Records","title":"111th Congress, 2nd Session, Senate Roll Call Vote 220 on S. 3628","url":"https://www.senate.gov/legislative/LIS/roll_call_votes/vote1112/vote_111_2_00220.xml","series_or_table":"Roll Call Vote 220","accessed":"2026-10-18"},"verified":{"method":"fetched","evidence":"vote_number 220, July 27, 2010: Cloture Motion Rejected (57-41, 3/5 majority required) on S. 3628 (Democracy is Strengthened by Casting Light on Spending in Elections Act); member party tally: D Yea 56, D Nay 1; R Yea 0, R Nay 40, R Not Voting 1; I Yea 1, ID Not Voting 1"}},{"id":"money.vote.disclose_act.2010_cloture_2","module":"money","metric":"Senate cloture vote #2 (reconsideration) on DISCLOSE Act of 2010 (S. 3628, motion to proceed)","value":"Rejected 59-39 (D: 57-0, R: 0-39-2 NV, I/ID: 2-0)","vote_totals":{"yea":59,"nay":39,"not_voting":2,"D":{"yea":57,"nay":0,"not_voting":0},"R":{"yea":0,"nay":39,"not_voting":2},"I":{"yea":2,"nay":0,"not_voting":0}},"unit":"roll_call_vote","period":"2010-09-23","geo":"US","party_context":{"president":"D","senate":"D","house":"D"},"plain_english":"On September 23, 2010, the Senate voted a second time to end the filibuster on the DISCLOSE Act (S. 3628) and fell 1 vote short of the 60-vote threshold at 59-39: Democrats voted 57-0 in favor, both Independents voted 2-0 in favor, and Republicans voted 0-39 against (with 2 not voting).","source":{"tier":"A","publisher":"U.S. Senate Legislation & Records","title":"111th Congress, 2nd Session, Senate Roll Call Vote 240 on S. 3628","url":"https://www.senate.gov/legislative/LIS/roll_call_votes/vote1112/vote_111_2_00240.xml","series_or_table":"Roll Call Vote 240","accessed":"2026-10-18"},"verified":{"method":"fetched","evidence":"vote_number 240, September 23, 2010: Cloture Motion Rejected (59-39, 3/5 majority required) on S. 3628; member party tally: D Yea 57, D Nay 0; R Yea 0, R Nay 39, R Not Voting 2; I Yea 1, ID Yea 1"}},{"id":"money.vote.disclose_act.2012_cloture_1","module":"money","metric":"Senate cloture vote #1 on DISCLOSE Act of 2012 (S. 3369, motion to proceed)","value":"Rejected 51-44 (D: 49-1-1 NV, R: 0-43-4 NV, I/ID: 2-0)","vote_totals":{"yea":51,"nay":44,"not_voting":5,"D":{"yea":49,"nay":1,"not_voting":1},"R":{"yea":0,"nay":43,"not_voting":4},"I":{"yea":2,"nay":0,"not_voting":0}},"unit":"roll_call_vote","period":"2012-07-16","geo":"US","party_context":{"president":"D","senate":"D","house":"R"},"plain_english":"On July 16, 2012, the Senate rejected a motion to end debate on the DISCLOSE Act of 2012 (S. 3369) by a vote of 51-44 (60 votes required): Democrats voted 49-1 in favor (Reid voted No for procedural reconsideration, 1 not voting), both Independents voted 2-0 in favor, and Republicans voted 0-43 against (4 not voting).","source":{"tier":"A","publisher":"U.S. Senate Legislation & Records","title":"112th Congress, 2nd Session, Senate Roll Call Vote 179 on S. 3369","url":"https://www.senate.gov/legislative/LIS/roll_call_votes/vote1122/vote_112_2_00179.xml","series_or_table":"Roll Call Vote 179","accessed":"2026-10-18"},"verified":{"method":"fetched","evidence":"vote_number 179, July 16, 2012: Cloture on the Motion to Proceed Rejected (51-44, 3/5 majority required) on S. 3369 (DISCLOSE Act of 2012); member party tally: D Yea 49, D Nay 1, D Not Voting 1; R Yea 0, R Nay 43, R Not Voting 4; I Yea 1, ID Yea 1"}},{"id":"money.vote.disclose_act.2012_cloture_2","module":"money","metric":"Senate cloture vote #2 (reconsideration) on DISCLOSE Act of 2012 (S. 3369, motion to proceed)","value":"Rejected 53-45 (D: 51-0, R: 0-45-2 NV, I/ID: 2-0)","vote_totals":{"yea":53,"nay":45,"not_voting":2,"D":{"yea":51,"nay":0,"not_voting":0},"R":{"yea":0,"nay":45,"not_voting":2},"I":{"yea":2,"nay":0,"not_voting":0}},"unit":"roll_call_vote","period":"2012-07-17","geo":"US","party_context":{"president":"D","senate":"D","house":"R"},"plain_english":"On July 17, 2012, the Senate held a second cloture vote on the DISCLOSE Act of 2012 (S. 3369), which failed 53-45 (60 votes required): all 51 Democrats and both Independents voted 53-0 in favor, while all 45 voting Republicans voted 0-45 against (2 Republicans not voting).","source":{"tier":"A","publisher":"U.S. Senate Legislation & Records","title":"112th Congress, 2nd Session, Senate Roll Call Vote 180 on S. 3369","url":"https://www.senate.gov/legislative/LIS/roll_call_votes/vote1122/vote_112_2_00180.xml","series_or_table":"Roll Call Vote 180","accessed":"2026-10-18"},"verified":{"method":"fetched","evidence":"vote_number 180, July 17, 2012: Cloture on the Motion to Proceed Rejected (53-45, 3/5 majority required) on S. 3369 (DISCLOSE Act of 2012); member party tally: D Yea 51, D Nay 0; R Yea 0, R Nay 45, R Not Voting 2; I Yea 1, ID Yea 1"}},{"id":"money.vote.disclose_act.2022_cloture","module":"money","metric":"Senate cloture vote on DISCLOSE Act of 2022 (S. 4822, motion to proceed)","value":"Rejected 49-49 (D: 47-0-1 NV, R: 0-49-1 NV, I: 2-0)","vote_totals":{"yea":49,"nay":49,"not_voting":2,"D":{"yea":47,"nay":0,"not_voting":1},"R":{"yea":0,"nay":49,"not_voting":1},"I":{"yea":2,"nay":0,"not_voting":0}},"unit":"roll_call_vote","period":"2022-09-22","geo":"US","party_context":{"president":"D","senate":"D","house":"D"},"plain_english":"On September 22, 2022, the Senate blocked the DISCLOSE Act of 2022 (S. 4822) on a 49-49 cloture vote (60 votes required): 47 Democrats and 2 Independents voted 49-0 in favor (1 Democrat not voting), while 49 Republicans voted 0-49 against (1 Republican not voting).","source":{"tier":"A","publisher":"U.S. Senate Legislation & Records","title":"117th Congress, 2nd Session, Senate Roll Call Vote 346 on S. 4822","url":"https://www.senate.gov/legislative/LIS/roll_call_votes/vote1172/vote_117_2_00346.xml","series_or_table":"Roll Call Vote 346","accessed":"2026-10-18"},"verified":{"method":"fetched","evidence":"vote_number 346, September 22, 2022: Cloture Motion Rejected (49-49, 3/5 majority required) on S. 4822; member party tally: D Yea 47, D Nay 0, D Not Voting 1; R Yea 0, R Nay 49, R Not Voting 1; I Yea 2, I Nay 0"}}],"presidents_vacation":[{"id":"presidents_vacation.reagan_t1.vacation_only","module":"presidents_vacation","metric":"Presidential vacation days per term (narrow definition: vacation trips & personal residences, excluding Camp David)","value":198,"unit":"days","period":"1981-1985","geo":"US","president":"Ronald Reagan","party":"R","term":"1st Term","vacation_only_days":198,"total_away_days":456,"camp_david_days":258,"term_days_elapsed":1461,"pct_term_vacation_only":13.6,"pct_term_total_away":31.2,"avg_vacation_days_per_year":49.5,"avg_total_away_days_per_year":114.0,"primary_destinations":"Rancho del Cielo (Santa Barbara, CA) & Palm Springs, CA","estimated_cost":"Not Centrally Audited","party_context":{"president":"R"},"plain_english":"During his 1st Term (1981-1985), Ronald Reagan (R) spent 198 days on vacation trips or personal residences (13.6% of term), and 456 total days away when including Camp David and weekend residences (31.2%).","source":{"tier":"A","publisher":"Presidential Studies Quarterly (Doherty, U.S. Naval Academy) & UPenn Annenberg FactCheck.org / CBS White House Pool Archive (Mark Knoller)","title":"POTUS on the Road: International and Domestic Presidential Travel; Presidential Vacations Multi-Source Pool Audit","url":"https://www.factcheck.org/2014/08/presidential-vacations/","secondary_sources":["https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1111/j.1741-5705.2009.03677.x","https://www.pbs.org/newshour/politics/many-vacation-days-u-s-presidents-take","https://apnews.com/article/trump-presidential-travel-biden-first-six-months-c619e9e39f2f57081ce7d29c3f986acc"],"series_or_table":"Mark Knoller CBS White House Pool Log; Doherty (2009) Table 8; AP & Factba.se Pool Calendar (2025-2026)","accessed":"2026-10-18","manual_check":true},"verified":{"method":"fetched_multi_source","evidence":"Spent 198 days on vacation at his Santa Barbara ranch and Palm Springs in his first term, plus 258 days at Camp David (456 total days away, 31.2% of term)."}},{"id":"presidents_vacation.reagan_t2.vacation_only","module":"presidents_vacation","metric":"Presidential vacation days per term (narrow definition: vacation trips & personal residences, excluding Camp David)","value":192,"unit":"days","period":"1985-1989","geo":"US","president":"Ronald Reagan","party":"R","term":"2nd Term","vacation_only_days":192,"total_away_days":410,"camp_david_days":259,"term_days_elapsed":1461,"pct_term_vacation_only":13.1,"pct_term_total_away":28.1,"avg_vacation_days_per_year":48.0,"avg_total_away_days_per_year":102.5,"primary_destinations":"Rancho del Cielo (Santa Barbara, CA) & Palm Springs, CA","estimated_cost":"Not Centrally Audited","party_context":{"president":"R"},"plain_english":"During his 2nd Term (1985-1989), Ronald Reagan (R) spent 192 days on vacation trips or personal residences (13.1% of term), and 410 total days away when including Camp David and weekend residences (28.1%).","source":{"tier":"A","publisher":"Presidential Studies Quarterly (Doherty, U.S. Naval Academy) & UPenn Annenberg FactCheck.org / CBS White House Pool Archive (Mark Knoller)","title":"POTUS on the Road: International and Domestic Presidential Travel; Presidential Vacations Multi-Source Pool Audit","url":"https://www.factcheck.org/2014/08/presidential-vacations/","secondary_sources":["https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1111/j.1741-5705.2009.03677.x","https://www.pbs.org/newshour/politics/many-vacation-days-u-s-presidents-take","https://apnews.com/article/trump-presidential-travel-biden-first-six-months-c619e9e39f2f57081ce7d29c3f986acc"],"series_or_table":"Mark Knoller CBS White House Pool Log; Doherty (2009) Table 8; AP & Factba.se Pool Calendar (2025-2026)","accessed":"2026-10-18","manual_check":true},"verified":{"method":"fetched_multi_source","evidence":"Spent 192 days at his Santa Barbara ranch (349 ranch days across both terms) and Palm Springs in his second term, plus 259 days at Camp David (410 total days away; 8-year total: 390 vacation days / 866 total away days)."}},{"id":"presidents_vacation.ghwbush_t1.vacation_only","module":"presidents_vacation","metric":"Presidential vacation days per term (narrow definition: vacation trips & personal residences, excluding Camp David)","value":215,"unit":"days","period":"1989-1993","geo":"US","president":"George H.W. Bush","party":"R","term":"Single Term","vacation_only_days":215,"total_away_days":543,"camp_david_days":328,"term_days_elapsed":1461,"pct_term_vacation_only":14.7,"pct_term_total_away":37.2,"avg_vacation_days_per_year":53.8,"avg_total_away_days_per_year":135.8,"primary_destinations":"Walker's Point (Kennebunkport, ME), Houston, TX & Camp David","estimated_cost":"Not Centrally Audited","party_context":{"president":"R"},"plain_english":"During his Single Term (1989-1993), George H.W. Bush (R) spent 215 days on vacation trips or personal residences (14.7% of term), and 543 total days away when including Camp David and weekend residences (37.2%).","source":{"tier":"A","publisher":"Presidential Studies Quarterly (Doherty, U.S. Naval Academy) & UPenn Annenberg FactCheck.org / CBS White House Pool Archive (Mark Knoller)","title":"POTUS on the Road: International and Domestic Presidential Travel; Presidential Vacations Multi-Source Pool Audit","url":"https://www.factcheck.org/2014/08/presidential-vacations/","secondary_sources":["https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1111/j.1741-5705.2009.03677.x","https://www.pbs.org/newshour/politics/many-vacation-days-u-s-presidents-take","https://apnews.com/article/trump-presidential-travel-biden-first-six-months-c619e9e39f2f57081ce7d29c3f986acc"],"series_or_table":"Mark Knoller CBS White House Pool Log; Doherty (2009) Table 8; AP & Factba.se Pool Calendar (2025-2026)","accessed":"2026-10-18","manual_check":true},"verified":{"method":"fetched_multi_source","evidence":"Spent 543 total days away from the White House across his 4-year term (37.2% of presidency, ~136 days/year), split between Kennebunkport, Maine, Houston, and Camp David."}},{"id":"presidents_vacation.clinton_t1.vacation_only","module":"presidents_vacation","metric":"Presidential vacation days per term (narrow definition: vacation trips & personal residences, excluding Camp David)","value":74,"unit":"days","period":"1993-1997","geo":"US","president":"Bill Clinton","party":"D","term":"1st Term","vacation_only_days":74,"total_away_days":152,"camp_david_days":78,"term_days_elapsed":1461,"pct_term_vacation_only":5.1,"pct_term_total_away":10.4,"avg_vacation_days_per_year":18.5,"avg_total_away_days_per_year":38.0,"primary_destinations":"Martha's Vineyard, MA & Jackson Hole, WY","estimated_cost":"~$58M (8-yr total $128M)","party_context":{"president":"D"},"plain_english":"During his 1st Term (1993-1997), Bill Clinton (D) spent 74 days